 *
 * ## Overview
 *
 * PicoKernel enforces a strict layer architecture. The **kernel is the
 * architectural center** every cross-layer request flows through it.
 * These rules are enforced at review time and are not negotiable.
 *
 * ## Architecture Diagrams
 *
 * ### Current Architecture (M10 Target)
 *
 * The diagram below reflects the architecture as it will stand at M10 the v1.0.0 milestone. All subsystems shown are either complete or actively in progress.
 *
 * @image html architecture-m10.svg "PicoKernel Architecture - M10 Target" width=70%
 *
 * ### Long-Term Vision
 *
 * The diagram below shows the planned post-v1 architecture, including
 * additional interface types, expanded module coverage, and hardware
 * targets beyond the current Pico 2W.
 *
 * @image html architecture-future.svg "PicoKernel Architecture - Long-Term Vision" width=100%
 *
 * ---
 *
 * ## Layer Model
 *
 * | Layer | Responsibility | May call |
 * |-------|---------------|----------|
 * | **Interfaces** | Outward-facing: web terminal, serial shell | Kernel APIs only (including scheduler) |
 * | **Modules** | Application subsystems: NFC, WiFi, packet capture | Kernel APIs only |
 * | **Kernel** | Allocator, scheduler, IPC, panic, state machine | Any required layer |
 * | **Drivers** | Thin hardware wrappers, zero business logic | Pico SDK only |
 * | **Pico SDK** | HAL: clocks, DMA, hardware registers | Hardware |
 *
 * ### Why this matters
 *
 * The layering is not just organizational, it is a **security and correctness boundary**.
 * A module cannot reach hardware directly. An interface cannot bypass the scheduler.
 * Driver code stays minimal and auditable. The kernel is the single choke point for
 * all hardware state changes. See @ref security_principles for the security argument.
 *
 * ---
 *
 * ## Architectural Rules
 *
 * These rules are enforced at review time. A PR that violates any rule
 * **will not merge** until the violation is corrected.
 *
 * | # | Rule |
 * |---|------|
 * | 1 | Interfaces never call Drivers directly. |
 * | 2 | Interfaces interact with the system through Kernel APIs only including scheduler registration. |
 * | 3 | Modules never call Drivers directly. |
 * | 4 | Modules interact with the system through Kernel APIs only. |
 * | 5 | Modules register their tasks with the Kernel Scheduler. |
 * | 6 | The Kernel Scheduler dispatches registered tasks. |
 * | 7 | The Kernel has exclusive ownership of Driver access. |
 * | 8 | Drivers call Pico SDK functions only, no business logic permitted. |
 * | 9 | The Pico SDK is the only path to hardware registers. |
 * | 10 | The Kernel is the architectural center. No other cross-layer channels exist. |
 * | 11 | Architecture violations are review blockers. No exceptions. |
 *
 * ---
 *
 * ## Architecture Philosophy
 *
 * ### The kernel-as-hub model
 *
 * Most embedded architectures let components talk to each other directly.
 * PicoKernel does not. Every inter-layer communication passes through the kernel.
 * This is intentional it means the kernel always has full visibility of system
 * state, and there is one place to audit, one place to secure, one place to debug.
 *
 * ### Drivers are thin
 *
 * A driver in PicoKernel does one thing: translate a kernel request into a Pico SDK
 * call. It holds no state. It makes no decisions. Business logic in a driver is a
 * review blocker. This keeps drivers auditable, testable, and replaceable.
 *
 * ### Modules are isolated
 *
 * A module registers tasks with the scheduler and communicates via IPC. It does
 * not know what other modules exist, what drivers are loaded, or what hardware
 * is present. This isolation is what allows modules to be added, removed, and
 * tested independently.
 *
 * ### Panic over corruption
 *
 * When a kernel invariant is violated, PicoKernel panics. There is no attempt
 * to recover from a state the kernel cannot verify. A visible panic with a
 * source location is always preferable to silent memory corruption.
 *
 * ---
 *
 * ## Subsystem Initials
 *
 * Used consistently in branch names, file prefixes, and Doxygen group names.
 *
 * | Initial | Subsystem |
 * |---------|-----------|
 * | `k` | Kernel |
 * | `d` | Drivers |
 * | `m` | Modules |
 * | `i` | Interface |
 *
